The [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/reports/chains_and_composites.html) managed by MITRE formally catalogs all variations of structural, memory, lifecycle, scope, and mathematical anomalies regarding variable handling. [1] 
The comprehensive list below encompasses every distinct architectural vulnerability involving variables categorized across software exploitation history: [1] 
------------------------------
## Group 1: Memory-Boundary Variable Flaws

* CWE-121: Stack-Based Buffer Overflow – Writing excess data past a variable's allocated boundary on the call stack.
* CWE-122: Heap-Based Buffer Overflow – Forcing dynamically allocated variable arrays to spill over into adjacent memory slots.
* CWE-124: Buffer Underwrite (Negative Indexing) – Referencing or writing to a variable pointer address located before the intended index array.
* CWE-787: Out-of-Bounds Write – The generic category for writing variable data outside the boundaries of the designated storage array.
* CWE-125: Out-of-Bounds Read – Allowing system processes to view contents outside a variable's bounds, causing an information leak. [2] 

## Group 2: Lifecycle & Allocation Flaws

* CWE-456: Missing Initialization of a Variable – The system leaves a variable unallocated, relying on unsafe or conditional declarations. [3] 
* CWE-457: Use of Uninitialized Variable – Reading from a declared variable before assigning a value, absorbing random residual memory junk. [2, 4] 
* CWE-908: Use of Uninitialized Resource – Utilizing systemic or structural entities before data parameters have been established. [5] 
* CWE-416: Use-After-Free (Dangling Pointer) – Accessing a variable reference pointer after its linked memory block has already been released back to the heap.
* CWE-415: Double Free – Executing a memory cleanup command twice on the same pointer variable, inducing systemic instability.
* CWE-562: Return of Stack Variable Address – A function terminates but leaves a pointer reference pointing down to local stack variable addresses. [6] 

## Group 3: Type & Cast Conversion Flaws

* CWE-843: Type Confusion – Allocating a variable space as one type but running code operations that parse it as an entirely different type.
* CWE-190: Integer Overflow / Wraparound – Incrementing a numeric variable past its bit limit, forcing it to instantly flip into negative numbers.
* CWE-191: Integer Underflow / Wraparound – Decrementing a numeric variable past zero, causing it to cycle into a massive positive value.
* CWE-195: Signed to Unsigned Conversion Error – Forcing a casting operation that changes negative integer variables into positive array bounds.
* CWE-704: Incorrect Type Conversion – Forcing variable conversion routines across unrelated object types, causing logic crashes.

## Group 4: Dynamic & Architectural Manipulation

* CWE-627: Dynamic Variable Evaluation – The execution engine treats raw strings supplied by users as actual variable assignments.
* CWE-914: Improper Control of Dynamically-Identified Variables – The program permits input parameter names to dynamically resolve into target application variables.
* CWE-473: PHP External Variable Modification (Variable Extraction) – Forcing input maps to overwrite pre-existing system configurations.
* CWE-154: Improper Neutralization of Variable Name Delimiters – Allowing specific symbol configurations to split variable structures apart.
* CWE-767: Access to Critical Private Variable via Public Method – Exposing secure variable fields through insufficiently protected getter methods. [7, 8, 9] 

## Group 5: Scope & Concurrency Flaws

* CWE-1126: Declaration of Variable with Unnecessarily Wide Scope – Exposing internal variable components globally rather than sealing them locally.
* CWE-1109: Use of Same Variable for Multiple Purposes – Reusing a single variable container globally across unrelated modules, crossing data streams.
* CWE-563: Assignment to Variable without Use – Storing values inside a memory variable container and leaving it dead, creating compiler bloat.
* CWE-567: Unsynchronized Access to Shared Data in a Multithreaded Context – Thread collisions modifying shared variable objects concurrently.
* CWE-367: Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use (TOCTOU) – A race condition where a variable's state shifts after a verification check passes.
* CWE-426: Untrusted Search Path – Relying on environment variables like $PATH that malicious local users can alter. [10, 11, 12, 13, 14] 

